1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
This network has fourteen stations. One cable, between station 2 and station 3, is the only link between the left region and the rest.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Cut it and the network falls into two pieces. An edge like this is called a bridge, or a cut edge.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Count them all: four bridges here. Every other edge sits inside a cycle, and no single one of them can split the network.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Inside each cycle the network survives any one failure. So the useful question is: which vertices still have two independent routes?

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Three small graphs, one test each: delete the highlighted edge and count the pieces.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Path of three: deleting the middle edge leaves two pieces, so it is a bridge. Triangle: deleting any edge leaves one piece, so none is.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:52,000
The same fact in one line: an edge is a bridge exactly when it lies on no cycle.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Boundary case: two parallel edges are a cycle of length two, so neither is a bridge - even though no simple cycle contains them.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Two routes from station 0 to station 2 that share no edge: one way round the triangle, and the other way round.

10
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Now ask it for every pair at once. Two stations are related when they have two edge-disjoint routes, or are the same station.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:23,000
The relation is an equivalence relation: reflexive, symmetric, and transitive. Transitivity is the one that needs a proof.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Its classes are the 2-edge-connected components. Here there are five, and the same five appear if you cut the bridges instead.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
A tempting substitute: call two stations related when some cycle contains both. On this bowtie it fails.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Station 1 shares the left cycle with station 0. Station 3 shares the right cycle with station 0. But 1 and 3 share no cycle.

15
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Yet the bowtie is one 2-edge-connected component: 1 and 3 do have two edge-disjoint routes, going opposite ways round the two triangles.

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
That is also the difference from blocks: the bowtie is one 2-edge-connected component but two biconnected blocks.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
A depth-first search from station 6 numbers every station as it arrives. These are the discovery numbers, d.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:16,000
The second number, low, is the smallest discovery number reachable from a station without crossing back over the edge it came in on.

19
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:24,000
The test: the edge from u down to child v is a bridge exactly when low of v is strictly greater than d of u.

20
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The one comparison that is easy to get wrong: skip the parent edge, not the parent station.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Delete the four bridges and the graph splits into five pieces. Each piece is 2-edge-connected on its own.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Now shrink each piece to a single dot and keep only the bridges. Five dots, four edges.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:54,000
The count identity: components after cutting the bridges equals connected components plus bridges. Here 5 = 1 + 4.

24
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
And the bridges of the graph are exactly the edges of that forest, so nothing is lost and nothing is invented.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
The bridge tree alone, drawn without the graph: a hub with three branches, one of which continues.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Three leaves: the left triangle, the square, and the third triangle. Leaves are exactly the places where one cut is enough to strand a region.

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Distance in the tree is the number of bridges on any path. Stations 0 and 13 are three dots apart, and every route between them crosses three bridges.

28
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Two stations in the same dot have two edge-disjoint routes; two stations in different dots have only one route at best.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Three leaves need two new cables: pair two leaves, and the third pairs with a leaf of the new cycle.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:46,000
The lower bound is a counting argument: each leaf must receive an added edge, and one added edge has two ends. So at least ceil(L/2).

31
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Checked by brute force: for every tree on four, five and six vertices, search all added-edge sets and the minimum is exactly ceil(L/2).

32
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
The formula is for the whole graph, not only trees: use the leaves of the bridge tree, and add that many edges between the right stations.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Bridge and articulation point are different objects. One edge with two stations: the edge is a bridge, and neither station is an articulation point.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
The other way round: the bowtie has an articulation point at the shared station and no bridge at all.

35
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:24,000
2-edge-connected is weaker than 2-vertex-connected: the bowtie is one 2-edge-connected component but two blocks.

36
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Third pair: strongly connected is for directed graphs, and on one undirected graph it can split into two while 2-edge-connectivity keeps it whole.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Step 1: decide what the question asks - which edges are weak, or which stations are safe from a single failure.

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Step 2: run one depth-first search with disc and low, skipping the parent edge by identity. Step 3: mark a tree edge a bridge when low of the child exceeds disc of the parent.

39
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Step 4: cut the bridges and label the pieces - that is the component list, and components equal connected components plus bridges.

40
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Step 5: for the number of added edges, build the bridge tree, count its leaves, and answer ceil of leaves over two.

